WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.180
INTRO:
People generally don’t have a very positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:09.120
opinion of Amazon the business. They may love the&nbsp;
service they offer and the convenience they offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:16.260
but more times than not, people aren’t exactly&nbsp;
fans of Jeff Bezos or how Amazon is run. The media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.120
often portrays Jeff Bezos as someone who makes&nbsp;
tens of billions while his everyday employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.540
break their backs for minimum wage. There is some&nbsp;
truth to this narrative given that Jeff Bezos does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.940
often make tens of billions every year, and his&nbsp;
employees do have terrible working conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.760
Their drivers are infamous for peeing in bottles&nbsp;
and their warehouse workers are often treated like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.980
disposable robots. One nuance that’s rarely&nbsp;
talked about though is the fact that Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:50.400
doesn’t actually make any money, and this has&nbsp;
been the case much of their entire history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.260
A few years ago, they were able to achieve&nbsp;
sustainable profitability with their retail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:01.020
business for the first time, but with inflation&nbsp;
skyrocketing, their retail businesses has fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:07.080
back into the red by quite an amount. In the first&nbsp;
6 months of 2022, Amazon was able to achieve an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:14.040
impressive $143.6 billion worth of sales in North&nbsp;
America. But, to achieve this number, they had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.740
spend $145.8 billion meaning that they lost $2.2&nbsp;
billion. Things aren’t much better internationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:26.520
either. Outside North America, Amazon was able&nbsp;
to achieve net sales of $55.8 billion, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:32.580
cost them $58.8 billion to fulfill these orders&nbsp;
meaning that they lost $3 billion internationally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.440
While Amazon’s executives like to play it calm&nbsp;
when they talk to the media or shareholders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.440
there is some evidence that they’re actually&nbsp;
scrambling internally. Likely the best example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.600
of this is all the rumors that they’re going to&nbsp;
hold a second Prime Day in October. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.400
they’re not gonna call it Prime Day though.&nbsp;
Instead, they’re gonna call it “Prime Early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.560
Access Sale”. Whatever they call it though, this&nbsp;
is clearly a move to capitalize on the holiday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.260
season and bolster their quarterly report.&nbsp;
But, why is Amazon’s retail business losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:09.060
money again in the first place and can the retail&nbsp;
business ever become sustainably profitable?

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:14.520
THE AMAZON DRAIN:&nbsp;

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.920
Fortunately for us, Amazon has been a public&nbsp;
company for a long time meaning that all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.160
financials for the past 25 years are all public.&nbsp;
By far the largest category in which Amazon spends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.000
money is actually acquiring the products the&nbsp;
products that they sell. If it’s an Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.460
branded product like a Kindle or an Alexa speaker,&nbsp;
this consists of directly paying suppliers for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.140
product and shipping it to their warehouses. If&nbsp;
it’s a private label brand or third party seller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.940
however, this is the portion of the revenue that&nbsp;
Amazon has to share with these parties. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:52.260
first 6 months of 2022, this number came out to&nbsp;
being $132.9 billion. This is not a bad thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:57.960
given that this is where you want Amazon to spend&nbsp;
money. You want to them to spend it on acquiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.820
good quality products, and by the looks of it,&nbsp;
they’re spending over half of their revenue on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:08.580
acquiring products, but this is actually super&nbsp;
misleading. You see, the variance in how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:14.100
Amazon has to share with their sellers is insane.&nbsp;
When it comes to large brands like Apple, Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:20.220
has to hand over well above 90% of the revenue&nbsp;
to Apple. But, when it comes to FBA sellers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:26.460
however, this number is usually closer to&nbsp;
20-30% if that. And this is what leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:32.040
the vast discrepancy in quality across Amazon.&nbsp;
Anyway, Amazon’s second most expensive category&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:38.400
is of course fulfillment. Shipping any product&nbsp;
to any household in America within 48 hours if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:44.880
not 24 hours is by no means a cheap endeavor. It&nbsp;
takes hundreds of thousands of warehouse workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.380
delivery drivers, pilots, trucks, planes,&nbsp;
warehouses, boxes, packaging materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:56.400
and who knows what else. Within the first half&nbsp;
of 2022, fullfillment cost Amazon $40.6 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.120
which works out to a little more than a sixth&nbsp;
of their revenue during the same time period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:06.300
This means that on average, whenever you buy&nbsp;
something on Amazon, roughly ⅙ of your purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:12.120
price literally goes towards just getting it to&nbsp;
you. Speak about expensive shipping. Moving on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.700
we have the technology and content category. This&nbsp;
includes all of the licensing fees that Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:23.040
has to pay for Prime movies and music. This also&nbsp;
includes development and maintenance expenses for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:29.820
things like Alexa and Twitch. Within the first 2&nbsp;
quarters of 2022, these added up to $32.9 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:35.820
And that brings us onto our final major expense&nbsp;
which is sales and marketing. Amazon is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.620
rather conservative when it comes to running&nbsp;
ads. Well technically, this category did add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:48.480
up to being $18.4 billion. But, in comparison to&nbsp;
their net sales, this is not even 10%. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.140
a few other categories that Amazon spends&nbsp;
money in like General and Administrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.840
and miscellaneous expenses, but these aren’t&nbsp;
particularly notable. General and administrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:04.560
came out to $5.5 billion while miscellaneous&nbsp;
expenses came out to $339 million. It’s really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.480
the first four categories that really weight&nbsp;
down Amazon’s bottomline. By the time, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.280
done paying for those categories, they either&nbsp;
have little money left or they’re in the red.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:22.200
THE AMAZON SAVIOR:
If Amazon’s retail business is at best a low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:27.600
margin business though, how did Amazon become a&nbsp;
trillion dollar company in the first place. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.840
this is not a new question by any means. In fact,&nbsp;
Jeff Bezos and his executive team were thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.860
about this not 5 or 10 years ago, but 22 years&nbsp;
ago. This is when the dotcom crash unfolded which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:47.880
essentially wiped out Amazon stock. Amazon stock&nbsp;
would crash 95%, but Amazon was actually one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:52.980
the lucky survivors. The vast majority of internet&nbsp;
companies straight up went bankrupt and shut down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:58.620
But, while Amazon survived this bloodbath, it was&nbsp;
clear to Jeff Bezos that they couldn’t just keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:04.320
betting on retail. If they truly wanted to become&nbsp;
a leader in retail, he knew that they had to spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.540
decades building up logistics, fulfillment, and&nbsp;
product diversity. And all of that would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:14.700
leave the business in the red. In the 1990s, Jeff&nbsp;
was able to use the hype surrounding internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:20.100
companies to raise extraordinary amounts of money&nbsp;
and keep the money pit afloat. But, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.620
dotcom crash, that was clearly no longer viable.&nbsp;
So, the executives started brainstorming about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.480
how they could sustain Amazon’s retail business.&nbsp;
They didn’t want to spend a lot of effort entering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.920
a completely different industry or inventing&nbsp;
something new because that would just take away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:39.720
from the retail business. So, they looked for ways&nbsp;
in which they could leverage what they already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:44.940
had. One of their first ideas was to create a&nbsp;
service called merchant.com that could help people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:50.820
create online stores. Amazon already had a lot of&nbsp;
experience building an ecommerce business, so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.200
figured they’d be pretty good at helping others&nbsp;
do the same thing. And given their reputation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.880
within ecommerce, they were actually able to&nbsp;
attract some high quality clients like Target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:05.160
But, very quickly, they realized that offering&nbsp;
this service was more of a full time gig rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:11.160
than a side gig. So, they shut down merchant.com&nbsp;
pretty quickly. Ironically, Shopify has since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:16.740
started a full on business around this, and at&nbsp;
the peak, they were worth over $200 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:22.560
But anyway, going back to the struggling Amazon,&nbsp;
they had to find new options. Leasing out their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.360
expertise wasn’t quite working, so they started&nbsp;
thinking about what else they could lease out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.040
They could lease out their fulfillment service,&nbsp;
and this is something that they would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:37.800
do, but as a startup, this was too big of an&nbsp;
undertaking. Something that was easy to lease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:43.140
out though was their computer infrastructure. They&nbsp;
had plenty of servers, databases, and networks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:49.560
What if they just leased these out? And that right&nbsp;
there marked the beginning Amazon Web Services or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:56.640
AWS. Most people have never heard of AWS and&nbsp;
will probably never buy an AWS offering. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:03.145
this is the secret that his been keeping Amazon&nbsp;
alive eversince it was launched in March of 2006.

00:08:03.145 --> 00:08:06.300
BAILOUT:
While Amazon’s retail margins aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.040
particularly impressive, the same cannot be said&nbsp;
about their cloud business. In terms of revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.620
AWS doesn’t pull in all that much in comparison&nbsp;
to Amazon’s total revenue. In the first 6 months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:24.120
of 2022, AWS pulled in $38.1 billion which works&nbsp;
out to roughly ⅙ of Amazon’s total revenue. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:29.940
AWS carries a monstrous operating margin of&nbsp;
32% which leaves them with an operating income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:37.320
of $12.2 billion. Now, compared to their total&nbsp;
revenue, this is still not that much, but it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:42.960
been enough to bailout out their retail business&nbsp;
for the past 20 years. Without AWS, Amazon would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.240
have lost about $5.2 billion from their retail&nbsp;
business within the first half of this year. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:54.780
with AWS, Amazon ended up profiting just under&nbsp;
$7 billion. Now of course, that’s just operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.780 --> 00:09:00.120
income. After Amazon wrote off $15 billion due&nbsp;
to losses from Rivian and other investments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:06.600
they were back in the red with a net loss of $5.8&nbsp;
billion. Also, we should mention that that’s with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:13.200
a $2 billion tax rebate. Without this rebate,&nbsp;
Amazon would have lost $7.9 billion. And if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:19.920
weren’t for AWS, that loss would have jumped up&nbsp;
to over $20 billion. Ironically, that’s about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.770
much Walmart profited in the most recent 12 month&nbsp;
period. So, clearly, Amazon’s retail business nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:30.780
their investments are performing especially well&nbsp;
right now. But, can Amazon turn things around?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:37.200
Well, it depends on how you define turn things&nbsp;
around. If you define it as simply can Amazon’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:43.020
retail business become profitable again, then&nbsp;
the answer is absolutely. As Amazon hikes Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:47.160
membership fees and FBA seller fees, they’re&nbsp;
essentially passing on inflation to customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:53.280
and sellers. And it’s just a matter of time until&nbsp;
they’re able to squeeze back into the green. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:59.700
if we define turn things around as becoming truly&nbsp;
profitable, then things aren’t so clear. Margins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:04.560
across the retail industry are generally&nbsp;
just not that great. Walmart, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.680
has dominated North American retail for decades,&nbsp;
but even Walmart is only able to achieve a 2.5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:16.920
net margin. Even at their peak, they weren’t able&nbsp;
to cross a 4% net margin. A similar trend can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:22.500
be seen with Target as well. Target was able to&nbsp;
reach a peak margin of 6.5% during the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:28.080
but that’s already coming back down to their&nbsp;
average of 4%. While Amazon doesn’t have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:32.640
pay for stores like these guys, Amazon ends up&nbsp;
spending all that money on fast home shipping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.740
so it really doesn’t make a difference. While&nbsp;
I don’t think Amazon’s retail business will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:42.360
perpetually lose money forever, I also have&nbsp;
a tough time ever seeing them cross even a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:47.940
10% net margin. So, we’ll probably never see&nbsp;
Amazon’s retail business reach the insane 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:54.120
30, 40% margins of their FAANG peers. But, that’s&nbsp;
just the reality of the industry that they’re in.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.580
THE FATE OF AMAZON:
Alright, boohoo, a massive corporation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:06.840
doesn’t actually make as much money as you would&nbsp;
htink. But, why does that even matter? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:12.240
the reason that that might matter is because of&nbsp;
Amazon stock. Amazon stock has historically always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.160
traded at a high PE ratio which is the ratio&nbsp;
between a company's earnings and their marketcap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:23.700
Mature companies generally trade for a PE ratio of&nbsp;
10, but Amazon is trading for a PE ratio of almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:30.240
110. Historically, this was tolerated because&nbsp;
Amazon was growing so fast. I mean, they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:36.000
yet to have a year over year decline in revenue,&nbsp;
but at the same time, it’s also clear that they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:42.240
starting to level out. This isn’t a bad thing,&nbsp;
it’s actually just natural. But, it also raises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:47.760
a lot of red flags as to whether Amazon should&nbsp;
have such a high PE ratio anymore. Investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:53.760
are starting to become weary and this is already&nbsp;
evident in the charts. Amazon was the only FAANG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:59.820
company who’s stock didn’t go vertical during 2020&nbsp;
and 2021. Their stock has basically been going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:07.320
sideways for over 4 years now. For perspective,&nbsp;
Apple stock is up 175% since 2018 even after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:12.960
accounting for the recent sell off. So, there’s&nbsp;
no doubt that Amazon stock has stalled. The real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:18.780
question is whether this is simply a pitstop or a&nbsp;
sign of something much more bearish. Are you a fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:25.020
of Amazon? Comment that down below. Also, drop&nbsp;
a like if you wish that this bear market would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:29.580
just be over already. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:33.420
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

